+## 8. Storage, Auth, and financial invariant boundaries
+
+### Auth and identity
+
+The live trigger links by email before verified-email assurance and can map an Auth subject to a legacy application profile; the live helper also returns the raw Auth UUID when no profile exists. The candidate changes this to no fallback, confirmed-user linking, exact legacy ID plus a server-controlled `raw_app_meta_data` verification marker, and client-profile creation as role `client`. The local backend adapter also stops looking up Auth users by matching email and does not reset an existing email-collision account. These are strong directionally correct protections, but actual GoTrue event timing, confirmation behavior, email-change synchronization, password reset/recovery, duplicate legacy mapping, session persistence, and first-admin provisioning remain untested on hosted Supabase. The candidate trigger listens to `INSERT OR UPDATE OF email_confirmed_at`; verify that real email-change/confirmation updates fire it as expected.
+
+### Reviews and public redaction
+
+The current public raw-table policy exposes `reviews.user_id`; candidate grants only display columns and exposes a `security_invoker` view with `id,user_name,lawyer_id,rating,comment,created_at,updated_at`. The view still exposes `user_name`, matching the existing Express display shape; confirm product privacy requirements before accepting public names. Candidate review submit derives the author from Auth; admin-only moderation/delete update the lawyer rating through a trigger. The PGlite suite checks that the view omits `user_id`, but no hosted PostgREST query was made.
+
+### Bookings, wallets, cases/tasks, subscriptions, chats
+
+- Booking creation derives price/name/owner/status in SQL, validates all advertised hourly slots, uses a unique slot key and idempotency index, and returns conflicts without partial rows. Cancel/delete release slots transactionally; admin reschedule is enclosed in a subtransaction so failure restores deleted locks. Availability rows are not locked, and no real multi-session race test ran.
+- Wallet function uses an advisory transaction lock plus `FOR UPDATE`, commits balance/ledger/idempotency together, rejects credit, checks currency/amount/overdraft, and is intended for authenticated self-debit. Payment-provider-confirmed credit and paid entitlement remain unavailable. The idempotency-key storage mismatch in CAND-03 is a release blocker for parity.
+- Case status uses `IS DISTINCT FROM` for NULL-safe ownership; admin case reassignment validates a real client/lawyer. Task status uses fixed transition arrays and row locking, stricter than the current direct Express task update contract. Status differences and legacy rows need approval/tests.
+- Subscription creation always produces `Pending`; no admin activation/status RPC exists and no signed payment webhook exists. Candidate prevents Active deletion for all roles; current Express allows only Admin to delete Active. Preserve history unless product/legal policy explicitly authorizes deletion.
+- Chat create/send derives identity and participant names from profiles; send locks the chat and updates message + summary atomically. Candidate's participant/admin DELETE policy is not usable without the missing table DELETE grant or an RPC.
+
+### PDF/Storage
+
+The client upload method uses the caller JWT/public key, an owner-scoped generated path, `application/pdf`, `x-upsert:false`, and a 10 MiB client check. Candidate bucket settings are private, 10 MiB, and `allowed_mime_types=['application/pdf']`; object path policies constrain `<owner>/documents/<opaque>.pdf` and grant authenticated SELECT/INSERT/DELETE, no UPDATE. Storage validates MIME metadata, not bytes. The Edge handler checks `%PDF-` after download when analysis is called and attempts cleanup; it does not keep arbitrary uploaded bytes from entering Storage first. No live bucket/object was created/read and no upload/delete was attempted. Test actual Storage API semantics (including bucket grants/internal API roles) on approved isolated staging.
